":"school","file":"layers/school.json","bytes":682503,"shapes":421,"good_to_zoom":11.8}],"school":{"files":"school/<id>.json"},"notes":{"lines":"Every ward line is the one its county sent the Legislature in July 2026, generalised by at most 1.5 metres and set on a grid of 0.00001 degree (about a metre). Cities, villages and towns, aldermanic districts and county supervisory districts are put together from whole wards. A point within a few metres of a line can fall on either side of it, and along a county line the two counties' own drawings can differ by several metres.","districts":"Which Assembly, Senate and congressional district a ward lies in is worked out here by laying the Legislature's district lines over the ward (analysis, not an official list): the district holding most of the ward's area. The few wards with 3% or more of their area in a second district are listed, and near such a line MyVote Wisconsin decides.","school":"School district lines are the Department of Public Instruction's. Which districts a ward lies in is analysis, not an official list: a district counts when its part of the ward is at least 60 metres thick somewhere; school_pct is each district's share of the ward's area (land and water, not voters). A union high school district lies over the elementary districts that feed it, so a ward there is in both and its shares add to more than 100.","authority":"For which ward an address votes in, and where, MyVote Wisconsin (the Wisconsin Elections Commission) and the municipal clerk are the authority.","precinct_ids":"A ward's id is LTSB's WARD_FIPS: 55, the county's three-digit code, the municipality's five-digit Census code and the four-character ward number. Clerks report results by reporting unit (one ward or several), which is not geography.","county_lines":"Wards fit together point for point inside a county and not across county lines, so the state, county, Court of Appeals, Assembly, Senate and congressional layers are drawn from statewide files (each layer's lines_from names its source) and can differ from the ward files by a few metres."},"sources":[{"id":"wi-ltsb-wards-2026-07","agency":"Wisconsin Legislature, Legislative Technology Services Bureau (LTSB)","title":"WI Municipal Wards (July 2026)","about":"Municipal ward lines collected from the 72 county clerks in July 2026 under Wis. Stat. 5.15(4)(br), with each ward's county, municipality, county supervisory district and aldermanic district.","url":"https://gis-ltsb.hub.arcgis.com/datasets/328b712cb66a420694ccbfe57aaffdda","service":"https://services1.arcgis.com/FDsAtKBk8Hy4cAH0/arcgis/rest/services/WI_Municipal_Wards_July/FeatureServer/0","fetched":"2026-10-02","sha256":"b071e54e06e2bf2d844a4f6db928eb89ef99553e3ba1b5c241d47e77fde107eb","rows":7161,"counties_sent":"2,534 wards came in on the commonest day, 7/1/2026","current_to":"2026-08-26"},{"id":"wi-ltsb-assembly-2024","agency":"Wisconsin Legislature, Legislative Technology Services Bureau (LTSB)","title":"WI Assembly Districts (2024), each with its Senate district (2023 Wisconsin Act 94)","url":"https://gis-ltsb.hub.arcgis.com/datasets/55e24ed7087e4d0492381713d770eafb","service":"https://services1.arcgis.com/FDsAtKBk8Hy4cAH0/arcgis/rest/services/WI_Assembly_Districts_2024/FeatureServer/0","fetched":"2026-10-02","sha256":"7007a44629992d82836351f41abc67de3cbc1429dfcca75f104e5004b6e11c33","rows":99,"current_to":"2025-09-09"},{"id":"wi-ltsb-congress-2022","agency":"Wisconsin Legislature, Legislative Technology Services Bureau (LTSB)","title":"WI Congressional Districts (2022)","url":"https://gis-ltsb.hub.arcgis.com/datasets/f42754138e2a4408810116e413e9947d","service":"https://services1.arcgis.com/FDsAtKBk8Hy4cAH0/arcgis/rest/services/WI_Congressional_Districts_%282022%29/FeatureServer/0","fetched":"2026-10-02","sha256":"d714ab133b1c20abe8f4c0dff7be975dd80fca05db2b136b348abe85f30224c5","rows":8,"current_to":"2024-09-27"},{"id":"wi-dpi-school-districts","agency":"Wisconsin Department of Public Inst